while 2018 is packed with a number of  highly anticipated big-budget games it's  also filled with a bunch of exciting new  indie games that you shouldn't miss out  on either there are so many hidden gems  to keep track of but here are five indie  titles that we've played and we can't  wait to see more of even if you aren't  familiar with the name Keita Takahashi  there's a good chance you've played his  most famous creation Katamari Damacy  that game solidified his reputation as a  developer with an eye for cute  characters and observed scenarios and  well tomm Takahashi's upcoming ps4 and  PC game fits the bill perfectly it's a  game about experimentation and discovery  where the act of play is valued over  completing explicit challenges some  could argue that it's not a game at all  so be it maybe what Tom deserves to fall  into a category all its own because it's  definitely unlike anything else around  playing as a mayor with a bomb under his  hat it's your job to create colorful  explosions to attract new citizens and  you've accomplished this by holding  hands with different objects and  characters in your environment the pre  explosion interactions produce many  kinds of unexpected surprises lending  with Tom a consistent charm that only  grows with each explosion has new  potential friends and sources of  entertainment enter the scene we've only  played a fraction of what Tom so far but  it instantly puts a smile on your face  and we can't wait to see what else  Takahashi has up his sleeve  [Music]  speaking of Katamari Damacy donut county  is a lot like that just in Reverse in  donut county you control a hole in the  ground that grows the more you can get  to fall inside of it start small with  lawn chairs and pebbles and work your  way up to buildings and even mountains  all while solving physics based puzzles  to fit everything in the whole donut  county is a solo project by indie  developer Ben Esposito who worked on  what remains of Edith Finch and the  unfinished Swan playing as a whole  sounds really silly and donut County's  colorful art and sassy animal characters  are cheekily charming but in practice  it's meditative and relaxing to play  we're excited to see more of what donut  county and its trash or treasures have  to offer and hopefully what's at the  bottom of that hole  [Music]  the gardens between uses some clever  tricks to present a story about  nostalgia and friendship between two  protagonists as a narrative focus puzzle  game each stage is presented as a  hodgepodge of memories referencing  moments and locations from their youth  maneuvering both characters past giant  constructs are the past you'll come to  understand that their memories hold the  deeper meaning one that examines their  bond and how it ultimately shapes their  future playing with time and perspective  move your character's forward and  backwards will affect time to solve the  many puzzles you'll often have to  separate the two characters and have  them tackle their own unique challenges  to help the other move forward but by  the end of every stage though unite and  move on to the next level  together the gardens between is  definitely one you'll want to keep an  eye out for in the future  [Music]  Celeste is a magical game that will  challenge you in a multitude of ways  it's platforming is really really hard  and you'll likely get frustrated at  you're fumbling fingers for failing a  jump or at your slow brain for not  figuring out how to get to the next safe  zone but when you get to that checkpoint  it's satisfying to know that your  fingers and brain are not in fact  useless more than platforming though  Celeste story is challenging the main  protagonist Madeline is faced with the  horrible journey both climbing a  mountain and battling her own mind and  at times it's not easy to watch her  suffer the game's writing is such that  it's easy to project that suffering onto  yourself and that can really make it  tough to face playing the next level but  you should absolutely do so it's a story  with an ending worth the struggle and  the cast of character is so endearing  you'll be rooting for them to succeed  just expect to fail a few leaps of faith  along the way waive the pacifist offers  a unique twist to the side scrolling  beat him up genre instead of relying on  standard button mashing to beat up  pixelated goons the game focuses on  timing based mechanics were you Parry  and dodge enemy attacks to defeat them  the result plays like a bizarre  lovechild between Streets of Rage and  Street Fighter 3 the action is fairly  straightforward enemies take turns  trying to fight you gradually teaching  you the varying pace of their attacks  before eventually tiring out and at  which point you simply push them over  while this fighting style sounds  counterintuitive for a beat em up it  works surprisingly well  the act of parrying and evading is easy  to execute it it offers a steady degree  of challenge especially against multiple  foes with different attack patterns add  that on top of a fist of the north star  like narrative and you have an action  game that's as charming as it is  mechanically distinct so those are the  five biggest indie games that we're  looking forward to this year but again  with so many to choose from  there are plenty that we didn't mention  if you know a game that people might be  sleeping on put in the comments below  and don't forget to visit GameSpot comm  for more coverage on all of these games  and more
 

